{"prompt": "A shirt costs $25 after a 20% discount was applied twice. What was the original price?", "response": "Let me work through this carefully. Two successive 20% discounts means the price was multiplied by 0.8 twice: original x 0.8 x 0.8 = original x 0.64. So original = 25 / 0.64 = 39.0625. Check: 39.0625 x 0.8 = 31.25, and 31.25 x 0.8 = 25. Correct. The original price was $39.06 (to the cent)."}
{"prompt": "If all Bloops are Razzies and no Razzies are Lazzies, can a Bloop be a Lazzie?", "response": "Step 1: every Bloop is a Razzie. Step 2: no Razzie is a Lazzie, so the sets Razzie and Lazzie are disjoint. Step 3: since Bloops sit entirely inside Razzies, and Razzies never overlap Lazzies, Bloops cannot overlap Lazzies either. So no — a Bloop cannot be a Lazzie."}
{"prompt": "A tank has a capacity of 500 liters. Water is flowing in at 30 liters per minute and draining out at 20 liters per minute. How long until the tank is full?", "response": "Let me think this through step by step. The net inflow is what matters here: 30 liters/min going in minus 20 liters/min going out equals 10 liters/min net gain. The tank needs 500 liters to fill. Time = volume / rate = 500 / 10 = 50 minutes. Let me verify: after 50 minutes, total in = 30 x 50 = 1500 liters, total out = 20 x 50 = 1000 liters, net = 500 liters. That matches. The answer is 50 minutes."}
{"prompt": "You have two coins: one fair and one double-headed. You pick one at random and flip it — it shows heads. What is the probability you picked the double-headed coin?", "response": "I need to use Bayes theorem here. Let FH = picked fair coin, DH = picked double-headed coin, H = observed heads. Priors: P(FH) = P(DH) = 1/2. Likelihoods: P(H|FH) = 1/2 (fair coin), P(H|DH) = 1 (double-headed always shows heads). By Bayes theorem: P(DH|H) = P(H|DH) * P(DH) / P(H) = (1 * 1/2) / (1 * 1/2 + 1/2 * 1/2) = 1/2 / 3/4 = 2/3. Quick sanity check: imagine 100 trials — 50 picks of fair (25 heads), 50 picks of double-headed (50 heads). Of the 75 heads total, 50 came from double-headed. 50/75 = 2/3. The probability is 2/3."}
{"prompt": "Three consecutive even integers sum to 90. What are they?", "response": "Let me define the integers systematically. If the middle even integer is n, then the three consecutive even integers are (n-2), n, and (n+2). Their sum: (n-2) + n + (n+2) = 3n. We know 3n = 90, so n = 30. That gives us 28, 30, and 32. Verification: 28 is even ✓, 30 is even ✓, 32 is even ✓, they are consecutive evens ✓, and 28+30+32 = 90 ✓. The three integers are 28, 30, and 32."}
{"prompt": "A recipe calls for 2 cups flour per 3 eggs. If I have 15 eggs, how much flour do I need?", "response": "This is a proportional reasoning problem. The ratio is 2 cups flour : 3 eggs. With 15 eggs, I need to find what multiple of 3 equals 15: 15 / 3 = 5. So I need 5 times the recipe amount. Flour needed = 2 cups × 5 = 10 cups. Let me double-check with cross-multiplication: 2/3 = x/15, so x = 2×15/3 = 30/3 = 10. Both methods agree. I need 10 cups of flour."}
{"prompt": "In a class of 30 students, 18 play soccer, 12 play basketball, and 5 play both. How many play neither sport?", "response": "I will use the inclusion-exclusion principle. Total students = 30. Students playing soccer only = 18 - 5 = 13. Students playing basketball only = 12 - 5 = 7. Students playing both = 5. Students playing at least one sport = soccer only + basketball only + both = 13 + 7 + 5 = 25. Alternatively: |S ∪ B| = |S| + |B| - |S ∩ B| = 18 + 12 - 5 = 25. Students playing neither = 30 - 25 = 5. Quick check: 13 (soccer only) + 7 (basketball only) + 5 (both) + 5 (neither) = 30 total. The answer is 5 students."}
